    "result_text": "Build is green \u2014 all tests pass.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city.\n\n**`Locality.java`** \u2014 added a postcode-first resolution alongside the existing city-only lookup, without touching the original `of(city)` method:\n- A small `BAND_REGION` map keyed on the postcode's hundreds band (`postcode / 100` \u2192 `20`=NSW, `30`=VIC, `40`=QLD). This exactly captures the ranges 2000-2099, 3000-3099, 4000-4099 with no loops or range arithmetic.\n- `private byPostcode(postcode)` \u2014 returns the region for a postcode, or `null` when absent or in no known band (CC\u22482).\n- `public of(city, postcode)` \u2014 uses the postcode region when present, else falls back to `of(city)` (CC=2).\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the single `locality` mapping expression now passes the postcode: `Locality.of(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())`.\n\n## Structural impact\n\nTwo files touched. The behaviour landed as two tiny new methods in `Locality`, a class with almost no surrounding complexity (WMC_other is just the trivial `of(city)`), so the postcode logic pays essentially nothing for context. The only edit to an existing method is the one-line mapper expression (an abstract MapStruct method, CC=1). No existing function grew in complexity, and `Postcode.java` was left untouched so its ranges and validation stay as-is.\n\nKnown cities return the same region as before; cities sharing a name are now disambiguated by postcode, and the new acceptance test (`3000` on an unknown city \u2192 `VIC`) confirms the postcode takes precedence.",
